  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...\n\nConditions will be warm and largely dry through midweek thanks \nto a building ridge of high pressure and a strong southwesterly \nflow aloft. Temperatures will rise into the 60s and low 70s this\nafternoon with sunshine mixed with some mid and high level \nclouds. Well above average temperatures more synonymous with \nmid- to-late September will continue through midweek along with \nmild nights. The peak of the warmth is expected to occur \nWednesday with 850mb temperatures between 12C and 15C and highs \nrange from the upper 60s to the mid 70s. A bit of a breeze is \nexpected to become more noticeable moving into Wednesday night \nand Thursday with an increasing pressure gradient over the \nregion. \n\nWhile conditions will be rain-free for most during the first \nhalf of the week, some showers cannot be ruled out on Tuesday. A\nsurface low pressure system will move over the Upper Midwest \nlater Monday. Extending from the low will be a warm front that \npartially lifts through the region. Depending on the placement \nof the front, some passing showers will be possible during the \nday on Tuesday, mainly across the Finger Lakes region and \nnorthern portions of Central NY.\n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...\n\nThe aforementioned surface low will push a cold front through \nthe region late in the week. Showers are expected to move in on \nThursday and continue into Friday. While the front may not fully\ndepart the region Friday as a another low pressure system forms\nover the Atlantic, the supporting upper trough is expected to \nmove from the west helping to keep the chance for showers around\nto end the work week. Guidance does suggest that a dry slot \nwill also move into the region on Friday, which can lead to a \nlull in the wet weather, especially during the midday and \nafternoon. \n\nWith a lingering upper low and the flow becoming more \nnorthwesterly to start the weekend Saturday, some additional \nlight rain showers are possible. \n\nFollowing the frontal system late in the week, temperatures \nwill trend cooler into the weekend. Even so, temperatures will \ngenerally remain above average for this time of year.",
